module Core

import Native

{-
   Ryu - Ulf Adams' algorithm, and the whole of what a native build does to decide a float's digits.

   Ported from Tritium's `FloatShow.cpp` and `FloatTables.cpp`, which are themselves ports of the
   reference implementation. It is integer arithmetic over a table of 128-bit powers of five and
   nothing else: no division by a float, no big-integer fallback, and a bounded number of steps for
   every input.

   **One algorithm for both widths.** `shortestOf` takes a mantissa and an exponent and knows nothing
   about which format they came out of; the two decoders below differ only in the three constants
   they use. The tables are the double's, which are wider than a float needs and therefore correct
   for one.

   A `.native.yana` file, so nothing here is even read on a JS build - Analysis-Modules.md §2.5.
   `Ryu.js.yana` beside it answers the same two names out of the host, and `Ryu.yana` holds the
   `Shortest` they both produce.
-}

{-
   The two tables - `FloatTables.cpp`, which is 362 lines of constants there and 315 here.

   Each entry is a 128-bit number, held as the two words `mulShift` reads: `[2q]` is the low half
   and `[2q + 1]` the high one. `pow5Split` holds 5^q rounded up to 128 bits and `pow5InvSplit`
   holds its reciprocal, which is what turns Ryu's division by a power of ten into a multiply.

   **`I64` and not `U64`, which is the one thing that changed in the port.** More than half of these
   have their top bit set, and a literal is read at `Long` - so `18446744073709551615` does not fit
   and would have to be written as an expression rather than a number. Written as the signed value
   with the same bits, every one of them is a literal again, and the one `bitcast` per read folds to
   nothing.

   `@platform(native)` because everything that reads them is: on JS the shortest decimal comes from
   the host, so these would be eleven kilobytes of data in a program that never looks at them.
-}
